<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Midrarid emirate</h1><p class="st-meta">757 CE – 976 CE (approx.) · capital Sijilmasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The emirate of Sijilmasa, a Saharan oasis city that grew rich as the northern terminus of the gold trade from Ghana.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> was founded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> oasis of south-eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-morocco">Morocco</a> in 757 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufriyya">Sufri</a> Kharijite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miknasa">Miknasa</a> tribe. Their first leader, Isa ibn Yazid, was deposed in 772, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-qasim-samgu">Abu al-Qasim Samgu</a> founded the line of the Banu Midrar, who gave the dynasty its name and ruled the city as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> for two centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a> was the chief northern market of trans-Saharan trade, where caravans brought gold from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a> to be exchanged for salt, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and textiles; its mint struck gold coins. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> kept ties with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rustamid-imamate">Rustamid imamate</a>. Between about 905 and 909 the future Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-al-mahdi-billah">Ubayd Allah</a> was held captive there until rescued by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-abdallah-al-shii">Abu Abdallah al-Shi'i</a>, who executed the emir al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yasa">Yasa</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Midrarids returned as Fatimid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>, but Muhammad ibn al-Fath adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni Islam</a> and a caliphal title, provoking the Fatimid general <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jawhar-state">Jawhar</a> to take the city in 958. In 976 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maghrawa-emirate-of-fez">Maghrawa emirate of Fez</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sijilmasa">Sijilmasa</a>, ending the dynasty.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 757 CE – 976 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">emirate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:74.77%;width:4.36%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>757 CE – 976 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sijilmasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abu al-Qasim Samgu, al-Yasa ibn Midrar, Muhammad ibn al-Fath</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sufri Kharijism, later Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Berber, Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Midrarids, Banu Midrar, Emirate of Sijilmasa</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=866&amp;focus=midrarid-emirate" aria-label="Midrarid emirate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4569 1038 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Midrarid emirate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4889 1294L5811 1337M4889 1294L4873 1200"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4889 1294h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4889 1294h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Sijilmasa. <a href="/atlas?year=866&amp;focus=midrarid-emirate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

